%T A399833 4,6,2,4,4,8,2,8,2,6,4,4,2,10,2,4,4,6,2,8,4,8,4,4,2,12,2,4,4,7,4,8,2,
%U A399833 6,4,8,2,8,2,4,4,6,4,8,2,10,4,4,2,12,4,4,4,8,2,8
%N A399833 The number of divisors d of n such that gcud(d, n/d) is a power of 2, where gcud is the greatest common unitary divisor.
%C A399833 First differs from A365207 at n = 27.
%C A399833 The sum of these divisors is A399839(n).
%H A399833 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A399833/b399833.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A399833 Multiplicative with a(2^e) = e+1, and a(p^e) = e if e is even, and e+1 if e is odd, for an odd prime p.
%F A399833 Dirichlet g.f.: ((1 + 1/2^s)/(1 + 1/2^s - 1/2^(2*s) + 1/2^(3*s))) * zeta(s) * zeta(2*s) * Product_{p prime} (1 + 1/p^s - 1/p^(2*s) + 1/p^(3*s)).
%t A399833 f[2, e_] := e + 1; f[p_, e_] := e + If[OddQ[e], 1, 0]; a[1] = 1; a[n_] := Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[n]; Array[a, 100]
%o A399833 (PARI) a(n) = {my(e = valuation(n, 2)); (e+1) * vecprod(apply(x -> x + x % 2, factor(n >> e)[, 2]));}
%Y A399833 Cf. A000079, A077610, A165430, A225174, A384244.
%Y A399833 Cf. A365207 (analogous with gcd), A399839.
%Y A399833 The number of divisors d of n such that gcud(d, n/d) is: A286324 (1), A399828 (squarefree), A399829 (powerful), A399830 (square), A399831 (exponentially odd), A399832 (odd), this sequence (power of 2).
%K A399833 nonn,mult,easy,new
%O A399833 1,2
%A A399833 _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 13 2026
